Version
tokio v1.53.1 from current master (460dc16d19b020a5601c118d167c2252c3729a91).
Description
Zero-length operations on DuplexStream and SimplexStream can return Pending:

a zero-capacity read when no data is available
an empty write when the internal buffer is full
an all-empty vectored write when the internal buffer is full

The same operations return Ready when data or buffer capacity happens to be available. Their completion therefore depends on stream state even though they cannot transfer any bytes. Both public stream types use the same internal SimplexStream, so the three cases have the same underlying cause.
Reproduction
[dependencies]
futures-util = "0.3"
tokio = { git = "https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio", rev = "460dc16d19b020a5601c118d167c2252c3729a91", features = ["io-util", "macros", "rt"] }
use futures_util::FutureExt;
use std::io::IoSlice;
use tokio::io::{duplex, AsyncReadExt, AsyncWriteExt};

#[tokio::main(flavor = "current_thread")]
async fn main() {
    let (mut reader, _peer) = duplex(1);
    let mut empty = [];
    dbg!(reader.read(&mut empty).now_or_never());

    let (mut writer, _peer) = duplex(1);
    writer.write_all(b"x").await.unwrap();
    dbg!(writer.write(&[]).now_or_never());

    let (mut writer, _peer) = duplex(1);
    writer.write_all(b"x").await.unwrap();
    let bufs = [IoSlice::new(&[]), IoSlice::new(&[])];
    dbg!(writer.write_vectored(&bufs).now_or_never());
}
All three calls print None. I expected Some(Ok(0)) in each case. These are first-poll results rather than timing-dependent timeouts. Awaiting any of the operations waits until another task changes the otherwise irrelevant stream state.
Why this appears incorrect
The AsyncRead documentation describes a zero-capacity buffer as a normal zero-byte Ready(Ok(())) outcome. This is also consistent with the empty File read fix in #7133 and #7139 and the zero-capacity handling added to Chain in #8251.
AsyncWrite::poll_write_vectored is documented to behave like poll_write over the concatenated buffers. The concatenation of all-empty buffers is itself empty.
The internal methods currently check data or buffer availability before checking whether the operation has zero capacity.
A narrow fix would return immediately for zero-capacity reads and writes. For writes, the existing closed-state check can remain first to preserve BrokenPipe precedence. Keeping the early returns in the internal methods also preserves the existing tracing and cooperative-budget wrappers.

Version
tokio v1.50.0
└── tokio-macros v2.6.1 (proc-macro)

Platform
Linux 7.0.0-22-generic #22-Ubuntu SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC x86_64 GNU/Linux

Description
After upgrading from Tokio 1.49.0 to 1.50.0, a spawned task containing tokio::time::sleep no longer completes when a current-thread runtime is driven via repeated block_on calls that are awakened from on_thread_park.
The same code works as expected on 1.49.0 and appears to have regressed in 1.50.0.
Minimal reproducer:
#!/usr/bin/env -S cargo +nightly -Zscript
---cargo
[dependencies]
tokio = { version = "=1.50.0", features = ["full"] }

[package]
edition = "2024"
---

use std::sync::{
    atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering},
    Arc,
};
use std::time::Duration;
use tokio::{runtime::Builder, select, sync::Notify};

fn main() {
    let notify = Arc::new(Notify::new());
    let task_done = Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(false));

    let rt = Builder::new_current_thread()
        .enable_all()
        .on_thread_park({
            let notify = notify.clone();
            move || {
                notify.notify_waiters();
            }
        })
        .build()
        .unwrap();

    rt.spawn({
        let task_done = task_done.clone();
        async move {
            tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(200)).await;
            task_done.store(true, Ordering::SeqCst);
        }
    });

    std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(20));

    for _ in 0..50 {
        rt.block_on(async {
            let n = notify.clone();
            select! {
                _ = n.notified() => {}
                _ = tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(200)) => {}
            }
        });

        std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(30));
    }

    assert!(
        task_done.load(Ordering::SeqCst),
        "spawned task should have completed"
    );
}
Expected behavior
The spawned task completes after approximately 200ms and the final assertion succeeds.
Actual behavior
The spawned task never observes its timer firing, task_done remains false, and the assertion fails.

  AI Investigation (maybe useful)

on_thread_park triggers Notify::notify_waiters().
This causes a wake-up while the runtime is preparing to park.
The runtime's woken flag becomes set.
has_pending_work() subsequently returns true.
As a result, park_internal() is skipped.
Since park_internal() is skipped, the driver is never parked/polled and timer events are not processed.

This seems related to the changes in PRs #7834 and #7835, specifically the optimization that avoids calling driver.unpark() for wakeups originating from within the runtime.

Am I relying on on_thread_park in a way that is not supported, or is this an unintended regression in the current-thread scheduler?
More generally, my goal is to periodically drive a current-thread runtime from an external event loop by calling block_on for short periods of time, allowing Tokio tasks, timers, and I/O to continue making progress without dedicating a thread to the runtime or blocking unnecessarily when no work is available.
Is there a recommended pattern for achieving this with the current scheduler behavior?

