Version
1.49
Description
I could be missing something, but it seems that when block_in_place is called, deferred wakers are woken up after the blocking call finishes and not before.
This may lead to indefinite stalling in (relatively) innocent-looking code.
E.g. consider there is task1 like this:
let (action_tx, action_rx) = mpsc::unbounded_channel();
...
loop {
    select! {
        Some(action) = action_rx.recv() => {
            do stuff and send back the result via a one-shot channel that is part of `action`
        }
        ...
    }
}

and there is task2 that sends an action to task1 and waits for the result using block_in_place.
What may happen (e.g. under heavy load) is:

task1's budget gets exhausted, so the select!'s future returns Pending and the waker is deferred.
the last time that task1's action channel was checked, it was non-empty, so no waker was registered by the channel itself.
task2 switches to the same thread that task1 is on.
task2 sends an action to task1 and uses block_in_place to wait for the result. However there is no way for task1 to be woken up before block_in_place finishes.

For example, this compiles even though I haven't imported the RotatorSelect trait anywhere:
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    tokio::join!(async {
        fn foo(_: impl RotatorSelect) {}
    });
}
Looking at the output of cargo expand on that example, I think the full set of symbols that gets leaked into the caller's scope is:
use ::tokio::macros::support::{
    maybe_done, poll_fn, Future, Pin, RotatorSelect,
};
use ::tokio::macros::support::Poll::{Ready, Pending};
